db/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1061 27847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23f94e12-bb19-5644-a2f6-54b67243d945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158164 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157317 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1061 27852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ff378f4-047d-5e9f-ba7d-3a98770af4f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158168 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1061 27933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b13c477a-3272-5b6f-bce5-91e1831d0674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158171 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158492 #edb/ref 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/c6/c64756e38cf4152fdbcd3c3cb3c99e6368b7ec2549f6ea96e6badedeb7607cd8.svg)\n\nIf a random variable ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) follows a geometric distribution with probability ![](images/f4/f43942df7283a860efbcb722d0d28b46eca1b4d2ffc58a91a1858e36b0947101.svg) then we write ![](images/d1/d196f25e129c7b7bb4bdfff7f0383f5fa79d352b82ca3a0d8634fe7a64e0dc8c.svg)\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is ![](images/f9/f98838fb4a565f05ea528dffcab8dad9e199e52bb3a2dfe071d6e11955e9ceb6.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) be the number of flips needed in order to get heads. Then ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/21/21bb1ed33c23f67b65a0c2337d7a34c17cd3e4f663b0b95737fff31bbafdf055.svg)\n\nIn particular, the probability of needing ![](images/6c/6cb44f25e489afb06ec62d2e95c4d235ece77eb36e2327b856264d2fb8b22c21.svg) tosses until we get heads is \n\n![](images/3c/3c965ff9951df068207847eb1c5eb105d486195f44f79adb654e43c8e50690c2.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158492 #edb/ref 1065 4527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158492 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d8664a30-c69c-59c8-9e1f-c2e46333f9be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158492 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158494 #edb/ref 1064 "The shape of the graph of the geometric distribution depends on the value of $p.$ When $p$ is large, the probabilities are concentrated at small values of $x.$ When $p$ is smaller, the probabilities are more spread out.\n\n![](images/51/51c0438105701a9ef6a19c280dde7bdddf6ccb82bd1ab487d2fdaf902bcca534.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158494 #edb/ref 1065 4535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158494 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4ed7d42f-4e9c-5a46-9270-1af8549e921d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158494 #edb/ref 1067 "The Graph of the Geometric Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
